#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Land this worktree's commits on origin/main.

    tools/land-run /tmp/<lane>-land.log  # detached user unit body
    tools/land --withdraw  # stop this checkout's queued or running land

Join the common git directory's FIFO ticket queue, then fetch and rebase
onto origin/main (the files many lanes add to merge with the
`hirsel` merge driver, tools/buck2/merging.py), regenerate the build files
(`sync.py --if-stale`), build the workspace, run Clippy and check formatting
through Buck2, then push. Every candidate passes fmt and line-list checks
before its build. The lane may keep working after submission; later commits
belong to its next ticket and its branch stays untouched. The queue head
lands a batch: its own commits, then
those of up to 15 waiting tickets in ticket order, checked once together in
a land-owned detached checkout and pushed in one push; a conflicting ticket leaves the batch,
and a red batch bisects until its culprits are found, pushing each green group
at once (docs/landing.md). A waiting ticket landed in a batch exits with its
own landed commits. A push rejected because main moved goes back to the rebase
with full jitter and no attempt limit. Row-only main moves reuse a batch's
passed checks after a clean rebase; every other proposed tree is checked in
full. Transient push failures retry the same checked commit once while main
is unchanged, fetching to resolve ambiguous completion. A red check
refuses the push and prints its first error.

Land checks every package and example manifest before pushing. The lane runs
the other tests its change can break, and
tools/main-runs runs a chosen suite or selection on current main when requested
by hand; it has no automatic schedule. Each push is appended
to `hirsel-landings.jsonl` in the common git directory, from which main-runs
names the lane of a commit.
"""

